      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n2\n0 1\n1 0\n4\n2 3 1 2\n1 3 1 3\n4\n0 1 3 0\n0 3 0 1\n4\n0 1 2 3\n0 3 2 1",
        "output": "1\n0\n2\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Suppose we choose i=1i = 1i=1, to swap A1A_1A1​ and B1B_1B1​. This results in A=[1,1]A = [1, 1]A=[1,1] and B=[0,0]B = [0, 0]B=[0,0].\nThis gives MEX(A)=0\\text{MEX}(A) = 0MEX(A)=0 and MEX(B)=1\\text{MEX}(B) = 1MEX(B)=1, so their maximum is 111. This is optimal.\nTest case 222: No swaps are needed: MEX(A)=MEX(B)=0\\text{MEX}(A) = \\text{MEX}(B) = 0MEX(A)=MEX(B)=0 already so their maximum is 000. This is optimal.\nTest case 333: No matter how the swaps are made, at least one of MEX(A)\\text{MEX}(A)MEX(A) or MEX(B)\\text{MEX}(B)MEX(B) will equal 222, so the answer is 222.\nTest case 444: One optimal solution is to swap A2A_2A2​ and B2B_2B2​, to obtain A=[0,3,2,3]A = [0, 3, 2, 3]A=[0,3,2,3] and B=[0,1,2,1]B = [0, 1, 2, 1]B=[0,1,2,1].\nThis gives MEX(A)=1\\text{MEX}(A) = 1MEX(A)=1 and MEX(B)=3\\text{MEX}(B) = 3MEX(B)=3, so max⁡(1,3)=3\\max(1, 3) = 3max(1,3)=3. We can't do better than this."
      }
    ]
